WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.440
INTRO:
Eversince America was created in 1776,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.720
New York has symbolized the American Dream. People&nbsp;
from all over the world would immigrate through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.840
Ellis Island hoping to create a new life in the&nbsp;
New York City. Overtime, this made NYC into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.600
heartland of America as the city became the home&nbsp;
of banks, brokerages, hedge funds, exchanges, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.320
basically every other financial institution you&nbsp;
could think of. We also saw the rise of Broadway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.880
and Carnegie hall which have made the state the&nbsp;
dream destination for aspiring artists as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.200
Despite this rich history, New York hasn’t been&nbsp;
doing all that well over the past few decades. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.120
fact, New York is the fastest shrinking state in&nbsp;
the US in terms of population. In 2021, New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.360
suffered a net population loss of 1.58% which was&nbsp;
nearly twice the population loss of second place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.480
This translates to a population decline&nbsp;
of over 300,000 people. Aside from people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.360
fleeing the state in masses, many have started to&nbsp;
associate New York with old wealth, corruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.360
lobbying, and deception. So,&nbsp;
what happened to New York?

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:07.280
HIGH COST OF LIVING:&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.320
Starting off with the most obvious reason, we&nbsp;
have the insanely high cost of living. Now, I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.200
sure you’ve all heard about this, but it’s hard to&nbsp;
visualize just how bad it is without some concrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.800
numbers, so let’s dive into some data. Also, I&nbsp;
should mention that most of the data that we’ll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.840
looking at will be regarding New York City. Nearly&nbsp;
half the state lives in this one city and it’s by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.720
far the number 1 place that people think of when&nbsp;
think New York. With that being said, let’s take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.560
look at housing. Right now, the median sale price&nbsp;
of single family homes, townhouses, and condos in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.640
NYC comes in at just over $800,000. Given that the&nbsp;
median income in the city is just over $41,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.560
the average house costs nearly 20 times the median&nbsp;
income. Even if we look at household income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.160
we only get $67,000 meaning that that the average&nbsp;
house costs 12 times the median household income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.280
This makes home ownership basically impossible&nbsp;
for the average person or family in NYC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.640
and that’s why most people don’t even consider&nbsp;
buying property in the city. Usually, most of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.840
just rent for their entire lives. And given real&nbsp;
estate ownership is responsible for creating 90%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.440
of all millionaires in the world, this is not&nbsp;
the best of signs for the people of NYC. To make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.040
things worse, it’s not like renting is that much&nbsp;
cheaper. A studio apartment itself will cost you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.440
$3,638 per month on average. That translates&nbsp;
to over $43,000 per year which is higher than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.800
median annual income in the city. And that’s just&nbsp;
a studio. If you want a 1 bedroom, you’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:42.160
at $4,761 per month or $57,000 per year. If you&nbsp;
want a 2 bedroom, you’re looking at $6,188 per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:49.040
month or $74,000 per year. And if you want a 3&nbsp;
bedroom, you’re looking at $6,544 per month or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.280
$78,000 per year. Obviously, none of these options&nbsp;
are affordable for the average resident in NYC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.640
I mean the rent for a 2 bedroom apartment is&nbsp;
literally a $1000 more expensive than the mortgage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.600
and tax that you would have to pay on a $800,000&nbsp;
house. If you could afford this by yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.360
you might as well just buy a house. Most people&nbsp;
in NYC can’t afford it and that’s why almost all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.440
of the average folks have roomates. In fact, 40%&nbsp;
of adult renters in New York live with roommates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.440
Aside from having to share your apartment and&nbsp;
still pay insane amounts in rent, you also have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:27.360
to deal with extremely old buildings. The average&nbsp;
residential building in NYC is literally 90 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.400
old. This not only makes these buildings dingy and&nbsp;
depressing but they’re usually infested with mice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.320
rats, and cockroaches. And all of that is just&nbsp;
the housing situation. Almost every facet of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:45.840
is just as expensive in NYC. Groceries for example&nbsp;
cost $486 per person per month on average. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:52.480
average across the US is only $348 or 30% lower.&nbsp;
Hearing all this, I don’t think you’d be surprised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.280
to hear that 60% of New Yorkers live paycheck to&nbsp;
paycheck. Clearly, the living situation for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.680
average New Yorker isn’t anything to brag about&nbsp;
and this has prompted many to ditch the state.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.920
THE RISE OF TECH:&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.080
Historically, one of the top reasons that&nbsp;
people immigrated to NYC was because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.560
all the job opportunities that were available in&nbsp;
the city. No other city in the world offered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.880
same networking capabilities when it came to the&nbsp;
financial markets or husting your way to the top.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.520
With the rise of technology over the past few&nbsp;
decades, however, the appeal of NYC has been on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.720
a decline. Take the stock market for example.&nbsp;
Before 2000, the trading floor of the New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.600
Stock Exchange was crucial to operating the stock&nbsp;
market. But, with the rise of the NASDAQ and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.720
electronic trading and execution, trading floors&nbsp;
have basically become obsolete. You no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.360
need to be in New York to make it in the financial&nbsp;
world. In fact, most of the fintech companies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.880
we hear about nowadays weren’t even created in New&nbsp;
York. This includes Robinhood, Coinbase, Square,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.920
Ally Bank, Acorns, Stripe, and so many more.&nbsp;
And if you don’t have to be in New York to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.480
build a financial career, why put up with&nbsp;
all the cons that come with the region. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.480
something else to note is that you no longer have&nbsp;
to be involved in the financial markets at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.520
to even become wealthy. A 100 years ago, the most&nbsp;
straight forward way to earn a high salary was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.400
go to New York, join a financial firm, and work up&nbsp;
the corporate ladder. But now, there’s an industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.360
that not only pays more but is also less intense&nbsp;
and less stressful and that’s of course tech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.720
Nowadays, the dream career for many is to get&nbsp;
a job at FAANG or an up and coming startup, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.880
this makes sense given how much these companies&nbsp;
pay. I’m sure you’ve all heard about software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.560
engineers making multiple 6 figures, but nowadays&nbsp;
top software engineers can even earn 7 figures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.480
At Google, for instance, even if you’re just an&nbsp;
entry level software engineer, you earn a total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:42.240
compensation of $191,000. Meanwhile, if you work&nbsp;
up to being a principal enigneer, you can earn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.800
a total compensation of $1.08 million. We can see&nbsp;
an extremely similar compensation plan at Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.120
as well. Even if you’re just an entry level&nbsp;
software engineer, you earn a total compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:59.280
of $184,000. Meanwhile, if you work up to being&nbsp;
a principal engineer, you can earn up to $1.61&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:05.280
million. Now of course, working up to principal&nbsp;
engineer is no easy task, but it’s also rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.400
achievable if you stick with such a company for&nbsp;
20 to 30 years. Also, even if you don’t make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.640
to level 8 and you get stuck at level 6 or 7,&nbsp;
you’re still pulling in well over half a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.440
per year, and that’s in today’s dollars.&nbsp;
By the time most of reach our 50s and 60s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.840
these positions themselves will likely be paying&nbsp;
well over a million dollars. So, finance is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.280
longer the most straight forward way to make big&nbsp;
money. Not to mention, while these tech jobs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.080
by no means easy, you probably won’t be working&nbsp;
80 to 100 hours per week either like investments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.720
bankers and hedge fund people. You’ll probably be&nbsp;
working closer to 60 hours which makes tech jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.200
even more appealing. So, the rise of tech has&nbsp;
robbed New York of much of its Wall Street appeal.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.600
NOT A FINAL DESTINATION:&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.520
Aside from a high cost of living and deteriorating&nbsp;
appeal, a major drawback of NYC is that it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.240
never really been a final destination. A lot of&nbsp;
people are attracted to the city by its fast life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:02.720
and career opportunities, but more times than&nbsp;
not, these people don’t intend to stay their for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.800
the rest of their lives. The vast majority of&nbsp;
individuals who are immigrating to New York can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.840
usually be categorized into two bins: the career&nbsp;
builders and the adventurers. The career builders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.160
usually come here straight out of college or&nbsp;
they might even come here for college itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.320
These guys usually get a job at a financial&nbsp;
firm or a consulting firm or they might even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.360
start they own company. Then, they grind out most&nbsp;
of their 20s in NYC and end up saving multiple 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.720
figures if not even 7 figures. And finally, they&nbsp;
move back to their home city or an up and coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.200
suburb where they get a job as a financial&nbsp;
analyst or accountant and start a family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.760
Meanwhile, the adventurers are broke throughout&nbsp;
their entire 20s, but they get the satisfaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.760
that they lived their lives to the fullest&nbsp;
while they were still young and free. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:49.920
these guys also usually end up moving to suburbs&nbsp;
as they grow older. In other words, NYC is rarely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.880
a final destination and this makes sense given&nbsp;
the pros and cons of the city. While the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.920
offers great career opportunities and a fast life,&nbsp;
it’s not exactly the best place to raise a family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.880
Cities in general usually don’t have great public&nbsp;
schools because they don’t get very good funding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.880
In fact, schools in urban districts get&nbsp;
$2,100 less per student than schools in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.200
suburban districts. Considering this, it’s not&nbsp;
surprising that parents are usually reluctant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.680
to send kids to public schools in major cities.&nbsp;
Even if you find a good school, the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:23.440
itself is usually not the best for kids. Crime&nbsp;
rates are high, subways aren’t the safest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.600
and there’s no neighborhood swimming pool&nbsp;
or park. And this is especially a problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.160
if you’re living in poorer areas of the city.&nbsp;
Aside from it being hard to raise a family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:37.040
New York isn’t the best place to settle down as&nbsp;
an individual either. First of all, the weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:42.320
is pretty bad. Winters are often brutal given that&nbsp;
New York receives the 7th most amount of snowfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:48.400
in the entire country. This is the exact opposite&nbsp;
of California. Californians often say that weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.120
is one of the top reasons that they wanna stay&nbsp;
in California. But maybe, they’re overstating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.800
this advantage given that people have been&nbsp;
moving to New York for centuries despite its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.160
bad weather. Anyway, aside from poor weather,&nbsp;
you also have taxes that will make you poor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.840
State income tax ranges from 4% up to 10.9%.&nbsp;
Combine this with federal income taxes and a high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.320
cost of living and you’ll be broke before&nbsp;
you know it. While this may be an acceptable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:17.680
sacrifice throughout your 20s, this gets old&nbsp;
real fast as you grow older. And sooner or later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:22.720
most people just want something more slow paced&nbsp;
and relaxed which usually leads them suburbs.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:27.120
THE FUTURE OF NEW YORK:&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.640
In the end, NYC has historically been one of the&nbsp;
most desirable cities in the world. People from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.120
all around the world have been immigrating to this&nbsp;
city since the beginning of America, and there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:41.520
still plenty of people moving to the city to&nbsp;
build their careers and lives. But, over the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.600
several years, the number of people leaving the&nbsp;
city and state has oupaced the number of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:51.040
moving to the state. There’s a slew of reasons for&nbsp;
this from bad weather to poor schools. But at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:56.080
end of the day, it just comes down to one reason.&nbsp;
Less and less people think the opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.560
offered by New York are worth the high cost.&nbsp;
Nowadays, you can progress within the financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:05.360
world without having to live in New York. Not to&nbsp;
mention, you don’t even have to join the financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:09.480
world to make large salaries anymore. Technology&nbsp;
and the internet have given people more high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.160
paying opportunities than ever before. The fact&nbsp;
that the billionaires list and the fortune 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.880
are dominated by tech is simply perfect evidence&nbsp;
of this. This isn’t to say that New York is gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:22.720
fall towards irrelevancy or something like&nbsp;
that either. New York is still filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.880
plenty of career opportunities some of which&nbsp;
are still unique to New York like consulting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:31.200
Also, there’s a lot of young souls who still want&nbsp;
to have an adventure in New York before the settle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:36.800
down in a regular old suburb. So, while New York&nbsp;
is the fastest shrinking state right now, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.360
just natural shifts due to changing opportunities.&nbsp;
Sooner or later, New York will settle down at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.560
new population level which will likely help with&nbsp;
overcongestion and affordability. And hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:50.800
this will open up the state to a whole new&nbsp;
generation of people, but that’s just what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.400
I think. Would you live in New York? Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if you’re all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.480
the chill suburban life. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:04.000
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
